<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The coverage threshold and the list of what it may skip. Kept in a file of
    its own so that a change to either is a diff nobody has to find inside a
    project file, and so the reason for each exclusion sits next to it.
  -->
  <Import Project="coverage.props" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Two entries because the plugin has two. This list follows the plugin's, so
      the suite runs on every framework the plugin ships on rather than on one
      of them while the other ships untested.
    -->
    <TargetFrameworks>net9.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <RootNamespace>Jellyfin.Plugin.Stats.Tests</RootNamespace>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>false</ImplicitUsings>
    <!--
      The tests are not part of what a user installs. IsPublishable keeps this
      project out of the publish output the packaging tool reads, and IsPackable
      keeps it from ever becoming a package of its own.
    -->
    <IsPublishable>false</IsPublishable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!--
      The suite targets the framework the plugin ships on, and a machine that
      only has a newer runtime installed can still run it. Without this the test
      host refuses to start anywhere the exact runtime is absent, and the repair
      would be installing a runtime rather than running the tests.
    -->
    <RollForward>LatestMajor</RollForward>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.8.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.5">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <!--
      The msbuild flavour rather than the collector, because only this one
      refuses. The collector writes a report and leaves the judgement to
      whatever reads it later, which on this repository is nothing; this one
      fails the test target below the threshold in coverage.props, so every
      route that runs the tests enforces it.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.msbuild" Version="10.0.1">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The plugin references these with ExcludeAssets runtime, because at a user's
    server they are the server's own assemblies and must not travel in the
    package. Here there is no server, so the suite carries them: without them
    the first touch of a plugin type fails to load MediaBrowser.Common. The
    versions are not the plugin's any more, and the difference is deliberate.

    The plugin references the floor of each line, in Directory.Build.props,
    because the floor is the only version every server on a line loads. The
    suite cannot follow it down on the 10.11 line: IUserManager in 10.11.0
    declares five members that 10.11.11 does not, so FakeUserManager, written
    against 10.11.11, does not compile against the floor.

        dotnet build Jellyfin.Plugin.Stats.Tests/Jellyfin.Plugin.Stats.Tests.csproj -f net9.0 -p:JellyfinFloorNet9=10.11.0
        error CS0535: FakeUserManager does not implement IUserManager.RenameUser(User, string)
        error CS0535: ... ResetPassword(User), ChangePassword(User, string), Users, UsersIds

    These are upgrades against the plugin's references, not downgrades, so
    NU1605 is not in play here; the direction that NU1605 refuses is the suite
    naming an older version than the plugin, and that is still refused.

    Moving the suite to the floor means growing the fake to the floor's
    interface, which is a change to the fakes rather than to the build, and it
    is written into issue #17 rather than done here.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net9.0'">
    <PackageReference Include="Jellyfin.Controller" Version="10.11.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Jellyfin.Model" Version="10.11.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net10.0'">
    <PackageReference Include="Jellyfin.Controller" Version="$(JellyfinFloorNet10)" />
    <PackageReference Include="Jellyfin.Model" Version="$(JellyfinFloorNet10)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The SQLite stack, for the same reason and with the opposite outcome. The
    plugin stopped shipping it on issue #330 and now compiles against the
    version its floor server carries, so nothing reaches this suite through the
    project reference and every store test failed to load Microsoft.Data.Sqlite
    until these two lines existed.

    Here the suite CAN follow the plugin down to the floor, unlike the Jellyfin
    references above: the floor package is referenced rather than a later one,
    so the assembly the tests load is the exact version a floor server supplies
    and the store is exercised against it rather than against something newer
    that happens to satisfy the same reference.

    The versions are read from Directory.Build.props, which is where both floors
    are written and the only place either is.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net9.0'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="$(SqliteFloorNet9)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net10.0'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="$(SqliteFloorNet10)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Jellyfin.Plugin.Stats\Jellyfin.Plugin.Stats.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
